lay, callback)` and `interval(delay, callback)` are effects returning `Result<EffectHandle>`; `sleep(delay)` is the future that fails with `Disposed` if the caller is disposed first; `ticks(delay)` returns `Ticks`, whose `tick().await` resolves on the next tick (instead of a `Stream`); `throttle` and `debounce` return `Scheduled<A>` handles. `ctx.timer()` (`TimerContext`) is shorthand for `ctx.get::<Timer>()`."],[8075,"The tracker/shadow proxy mechanism (Services and traceability) attributes effects created inside service methods to the **calling** fiber and resolves the service's own dependencies from its **def site**. The paper's confinement (Def. 55) implicitly assumes this; without it, service-mediated effects would belong to the provider and break temporal composability for consumers."],[6284,"The transaction itself fails (a bad root, a nested transaction, a failing callback)"],[7559,"the transition in flight"],[7496,"The transition state machine"],[7501,"The two guards"],[4582,"The two-layer resolution `k → ρ(k) → σ(ρ(k))` decouples the logical layer from storage. The paper describes this as **runtime ad-hoc polymorphism**: the same key resolves to different values in different contexts, adjustable at runtime (multi-tenant systems, testing, sandboxes). `set` remains an effect function (revertible); `isolate` needs no inverse."],[6611,"The type `P`: every call gives the same identity"],[6737,"the type of its context parameter, `Injected<(A, B)>`"],[6593,"The typed accesses return `Svc<S>`, which dereferences to `S` and remembers the accessing context (the caller). Services such as `timer` use the caller to make what they do on your behalf an effect of *your* plugin; Services explains caller attribution, interception and events."],[6945,"The TypeScript runtime"],[5296,"The TypeScript side"],[7937,"The unregister-before-dispose ordering in `remove`/`_reject` is what lets self-dispose detection distinguish loader-driven disposal."],[5742,"The values of `intercept`