ivates only the dependents whose resolved dependency changed; a plugin with an unavailable dependency waits without erroring."],[6201,"There is no `cordis_call`, as the plugin provides no service."],[5029,"There is no `NPM_TOKEN`. If trusted publishing ever has to be bypassed, a granular npm access token limited to the `@kardiajs` packages, stored as an `npm` environment secret, would be the fallback; the workflow does not use one."],[6940,"There is no `tracing` bridge; the plan leaves a `tracing_subscriber::Layer` feeding exporters as an optional addition to `kardia-logger-console`."],[6729,"There is no in-place overwrite of a bound value. To change what consumers see, provide a new value (withdraw and re-provide, restart or update the provider), so that the change is observed."],[7678,"There is no loader-specific code: the loader imports plugins through the same internal ESM loader, so their modules are in the cache hmr inspects. hmr replaces plugins at the **registry** level, and loader entries keep their fibers."],[7185,"These packages are not described in the paper; they show how ordinary services are built on the core primitives."],[8186,"These pages describe Cordis, not kardia. Names such as `Context`, `Fiber`, `ctx.effect` and paths such as `cordis/packages/core/src/fiber.ts` refer to the TypeScript code. How kardia maps them to Rust is covered in the Development section."],[5473,"These tests check the implementation against the theory with no business logic copied into the tests."],[7978,"Things the paper assumes that the code does not check"],[7158,"Things to try while it runs"],[6998,"Third-party UI code runs with the webview's full access to Tauri: load only trusted code into the main webview, and give untrusted extensions declarative contributions or a sandboxed webview."],[7289,"This is Algorithm 3. The *filter* parameter exists so that the loader can replace the realm test during realm reassignment (Algorithm 7, see Managed realms)."],[4648,"This is an open problem."],[7324,"This is exactly `σ(k)(d(k) ⊕ ι(k))` from Definition 27, with ⊕ = shallow object merge (customizable per service). The `logger` service uses it for `{ name, level }` (`logger.ts:213-224`)."],[8157,"This is how independent plugins extend each other's types at runtime: the dynamic counterpart of TypeScript module augmentation (paper §6.4, see Discussion and related work)."],[6973,"This is the arrangement the paper describes for crossing a process boundary (each process hosts its own context, and calls between them follow an asynchronous contract, see Related work)."],[5104,"This is the loader's outer-fiber pattern (`yield () => {}` after import) made explicit."],[4204,"This is well defined because `dom(σ_n) ⊆ p_n` and the provisions of distinct fibers are disjoint (O-Insert enforces it). Each key thus has at most one possible provider, `provider_k(γ)`."],[4804,"this page"],[7981,"This page compares the theory (the paper's model and its pseudo-code, §3–5; see Concepts) with the implementation (the TypeScript code at `cordis/`). It is the bridge used by the Rust design: where the two differ, the Development pages state which one kardia follows and why."],[7247,"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/context.ts`, `reflect.ts` and `utils.ts`. The paper counterparts are §3.2 (coeffects, see Reactive coeffects), §5.1.2 (Algorithms 2–3) and §5.1.4 (Algorithm 6), both in Implementation."],[7413,"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/events.ts` and `logger.ts`. In the paper, event listeners are the canonical *commutative key* (\"registration takes an entry of its own\", §3.4.2, see The context paradigm); the logger is an example of interception metadata."],[7339,"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/fiber.ts:60-363` and `utils.ts`. The paper counterpart is §3.1 and Algorithm 1; see Revertible effects."],[7505,"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/fiber.ts` and `registry.ts`. The paper counterparts are §4.1–4.2 (the calculus, see Calculus) and §5.1.3 (Algorithms 4–5, see Implementation)."],[8114,"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/service.ts` and `utils.ts:113-238`. The paper counterparts are §5.1.4 (context access, see Implementation) and §6.3 (capabilities, see Discussion and related work). The mechanism is mostly *implicit* in the paper, but it is essential for temporal composability in practice."],[7657,"This page covers `cordis/packages/hmr/src/index.ts` and `error.ts`, plus `core/src/registry.ts:185-209` and `core/src/fiber.ts:401-416` (see `registry.replace`). The paper counterpart is §5.2.2, Algorithms 8–10; see Implementation."],[7741,"This page covers `cordis/packages/include/src/{index,journal,patch}.ts` and `packages/group/src/index.ts`. The paper counterpart is §5.2.1 (\"`@cordisjs/include` loads an external configuration file (YAML or JSON) and grafts its entries in as a nested subtree\", see Implementation). The paper does not describe the synchronization machinery below; it is an implementation contribution."],[7827,"This page covers `cordis/packages/loader/src/**`. The paper counterparts are §5.2.1 *Declarative configuration* (see Implementation) and §4.4 *Configuration* (see Metatheory). File:line references on this page are relative to `packages/loader/src/`."],[5062,"This page covers the public API of `kardia-core` and the built-in plugin crates: services and access (decisions D3, D4), effects (D5, D6), plugins, configuration and the lifecycle (D7), and events, the logger and built-in services. The data structures these operate on are on Core data structures. For a user-oriented introduction, see the guide pages Plugins, Services and Effects."],[4890,"This page is decision **D1** of the guiding decisions: a `Send + Sync` core with one state lock per root, in which no user code runs while the lock is held."],[6311,"This page is the library user's view: how to set the loader up, drive it from Rust, include files and find config errors. The entry fields themselves (`id`